{"assets":["/statement-assets/3f315575a3cbbabcac33a3ef63279cafa4ff2f9fed7af1bfc4c5a05d76ade75e.png","/statement-assets/e15868a8de21f8aa2a1cff04f30e47ecd041e0515fbd6d4ce7041c2285fcbb13.png","/statement-assets/7a53b4b0a9bb27cdf73f4f271996cb4934542ec4714191b5d308b5f7ad709fa3.png","/statement-assets/9b110f456124e9e036e8a9c0d5d464fcc75b8fa539fc57686a2347b40fb8837e.png","/statement-assets/014c33424df16678dd31ae6c6a1a650b060a6f9a550431fddadf51e7b00018f8.png","/statement-assets/c8ed0af7782356298b721b4c7ea092bac478a956adc802f9d4fc0d4223db36bc.png","/statement-assets/d999757ff2ce7285a0bc78f2ee13c6626ebe715c6d11fb7b27cc3077877401f0.png"],"contentKind":"statement","contestId":"2178","diagnostics":[],"document":{"children":[{"attrs":{"level":1,"role":"title"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"I. Numbers or Fireworks"}],"kind":"heading"},{"attrs":{"role":"time_limit"},"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"time limit per test"}],"kind":"container"},{"kind":"text","text":"12 seconds"}],"kind":"container"},{"attrs":{"role":"memory_limit"},"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"memory limit per test"}],"kind":"container"},{"kind":"text","text":"1024 megabytes"}],"kind":"container"},{"attrs":{"role":"body"},"children":[{"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Now that all the presents have been delivered, the North Pole is finally at peace — and it's time to celebrate with a spectacular New Year's fireworks show!"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The North Pole is modeled as the Cartesian plane, with $$$n$$$ cities located at distinct lattice points. You are also given an integer $$$k$$$."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"For a proper subset$$$^{\\text{∗}}$$$ $$$T$$$ of the cities ($$$T$$$ may be empty), we define its "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"explosiveness"}],"kind":"container"},{"kind":"text","text":" as follows: "}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"ordered":true},"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":" A firework is launched from every city in $$$T$$$. "}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":" For each city $$$c$$$ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"not"}],"kind":"container"},{"kind":"text","text":" in $$$T$$$, let $$$f$$$ be the number of fireworks launched from cities that are exactly $$$\\sqrt{k}$$$ Euclidean distance$$$^{\\text{†}}$$$ away from $$$c$$$. Assign the number $$$(f+1)$$$ to this city $$$c$$$. "}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":" The "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"explosiveness"}],"kind":"container"},{"kind":"text","text":" of $$$T$$$ is the product of all numbers assigned in Step 2. "}],"kind":"list_item"}],"kind":"list"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Compute the sum of the "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"explosiveness"}],"kind":"container"},{"kind":"text","text":" of $$$T$$$ over all $$$2^n-1$$$ proper subsets $$$T$$$ of the cities. Since the answer may be large, output it modulo $$$998\\,244\\,353$$$."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"$$$^{\\text{∗}}$$$A proper subset is any subset which is not equal to the whole set."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"$$$^{\\text{†}}$$$The Euclidean distance between two points $$$(x, y)$$$ and $$$(p, q)$$$ is defined as $$$\\sqrt{(x - p)^2 + (y - q)^2}$$$."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"section"},{"attrs":{"role":"input_specification"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":2},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Input"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Each test contains multiple test cases. The first line contains the number of test cases $$$t$$$ ($$$1 \\le t \\le 1000$$$). The description of the test cases follows. "}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The first line of each test case contains two integers $$$n$$$ and $$$k$$$ ($$$2\\le n\\le 31$$$, $$$1\\leq k \\leq2\\cdot 10^4$$$)."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Then $$$n$$$ lines follow, the $$$i$$$-th line containing two integers $$$x_i$$$ and $$$y_i$$$ ($$$1\\leq x_i, y_i\\leq 100$$$) — the $$$x$$$ and $$$y$$$ coordinates of the $$$i$$$-th city, respectively. It is guaranteed that the coordinates of the cities are pairwise distinct. "}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"It is guaranteed that the sum of $$$n^3$$$ over all test cases does not exceed $$$31^3$$$. "}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"section"},{"attrs":{"role":"output_specification"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":2},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Output"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"For each test case, print a single integer — the sum, modulo $$$998\\,244\\,353$$$, of the "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"explosiveness"}],"kind":"container"},{"kind":"text","text":" over all proper subsets $$$T$$$ of the cities."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"section"},{"attrs":{"role":"samples"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":2},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Example"}],"kind":"heading"},{"attrs":{"role":"sample"},"children":[{"attrs":{"role":"sample_input"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Input"}],"kind":"heading"},{"kind":"code_block","text":"\n4\n3 1\n1 2\n2 1\n2 2\n2 20000\n1 1\n100 100\n9 5\n1 1\n1 2\n1 3\n2 1\n2 2\n2 3\n3 1\n3 2\n3 3\n13 25\n50 50\n55 50\n54 53\n53 54\n50 55\n47 54\n46 53\n45 50\n46 47\n47 46\n50 45\n53 46\n54 47\n"}],"kind":"section"},{"attrs":{"role":"sample_output"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Output"}],"kind":"heading"},{"kind":"code_block","text":"\n16\n3\n8255\n560112\n"}],"kind":"section"}],"kind":"section"}],"kind":"section"},{"attrs":{"role":"note"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":2},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Note"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"In the first test case, below are the $$$2^3-1=7$$$ possible placements of fireworks. The numbers assigned to cities not in $$$T$$$ are circled. "}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"attrs":{"header":false},"children":[{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/statement-assets/3f315575a3cbbabcac33a3ef63279cafa4ff2f9fed7af1bfc4c5a05d76ade75e.png"},"kind":"image"}],"kind":"table_cell"},{"attrs":{"header":false},"children":[{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/statement-assets/e15868a8de21f8aa2a1cff04f30e47ecd041e0515fbd6d4ce7041c2285fcbb13.png"},"kind":"image"}],"kind":"table_cell"},{"attrs":{"header":false},"children":[{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/statement-assets/7a53b4b0a9bb27cdf73f4f271996cb4934542ec4714191b5d308b5f7ad709fa3.png"},"kind":"image"}],"kind":"table_cell"}],"kind":"table_row"},{"children":[{"attrs":{"header":false},"children":[{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/statement-assets/9b110f456124e9e036e8a9c0d5d464fcc75b8fa539fc57686a2347b40fb8837e.png"},"kind":"image"}],"kind":"table_cell"},{"attrs":{"header":false},"children":[{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/statement-assets/014c33424df16678dd31ae6c6a1a650b060a6f9a550431fddadf51e7b00018f8.png"},"kind":"image"}],"kind":"table_cell"},{"attrs":{"header":false},"children":[{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/statement-assets/c8ed0af7782356298b721b4c7ea092bac478a956adc802f9d4fc0d4223db36bc.png"},"kind":"image"}],"kind":"table_cell"}],"kind":"table_row"},{"children":[{"attrs":{"header":false},"children":[{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/statement-assets/d999757ff2ce7285a0bc78f2ee13c6626ebe715c6d11fb7b27cc3077877401f0.png"},"kind":"image"}],"kind":"table_cell"},{"attrs":{"header":false},"kind":"table_cell"},{"attrs":{"header":false},"kind":"table_cell"}],"kind":"table_row"}],"kind":"table_body"}],"kind":"table"},{"kind":"text","text":" The total "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"explosiveness"}],"kind":"container"},{"kind":"text","text":" is $$$(1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1)+(1\\cdot 2)+(2\\cdot 1)+(2\\cdot 2)+(3)+(2)+(2)=16$$$."},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"In the second test case, there are $$$2^2-1=3$$$ possible placements of fireworks. For every placement, since $$$(1,1)$$$ and $$$(100,100)$$$ have a Euclidean distance of $$$\\sqrt{(100-1)^2+(100-1)^2}=\\sqrt{19\\,602}\\neq\\sqrt{20\\,000}$$$, none of the cities will have fireworks that are exactly $$$\\sqrt{k}$$$ away. Thus, the number $$$1$$$ is assigned to every city without fireworks. Therefore, the "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"explosiveness"}],"kind":"container"},{"kind":"text","text":" is $$$1$$$ for every $$$T$$$, and the answer is $$$1+1+1=3$$$."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"section"}],"kind":"document"},"index":"I","problemCode":"2178I","schema":2,"sourceKind":"html","sourceUrl":"https://codeforces.com/contest/2178/problem/I"}